# Line endings, decided here rather than by whoever happens to clone the repository.
#
# Everything is already stored LF, and stays that way. What this changes is the working tree on
# Windows, where Git for Windows' core.autocrlf=true checks every text file out as CRLF - and that
# is not merely cosmetic. A C# raw string literal keeps whatever line endings its .cs file has, so
# the Ollama prompt built from a Windows checkout came out CRLF where the same source on Linux gave
# LF: the same commit, different bytes sent to the model, and one test that could only fail here.
#
# LF in the working tree on every platform. The .NET SDK, PowerShell, bash and every editor in use
# here read LF happily on Windows; the shell scripts and systemd units that get copied to a Linux
# box do not read CRLF happily at all.

* text=auto eol=lf

# The one thing that genuinely wants CRLF: cmd.exe can mis-parse labels and multi-line blocks
# otherwise. Naming it here also normalises what is stored, which is why gradlew.bat changes.
*.bat text eol=crlf
*.cmd text eol=crlf

# Never converted, never diffed as text.
*.png   binary
*.jpg   binary
*.jpeg  binary
*.gif   binary
*.ico   binary
*.ttf   binary
*.otf   binary
*.woff  binary
*.woff2 binary
*.onnx  binary
*.jar   binary
*.keystore binary
*.pdf   binary
*.zip   binary
*.mp4   binary
